		<main>

			<!-- ==================================================
			     HERO
			================================================== -->

			<section class="paper-hero">

				<div class="hero-meta">

					<span>
						Preprint
					</span>

					<span>
						Combinatorics · 2026
					</span>

				</div>


				<h1>
					Counterexamples regarding
					elementary symmetric partitions
				</h1>


				<div class="hero-authors">

					<p>
						Vixail Hadelyn
					</p>

					<p>
						Harper Niergarth
					</p>

					<p class="me">
						Weiyou Li
					</p>

					<p>
						Wenhui Li
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="hero-bottom">

					<div class="hero-description">

						<p>
							A combinatorics preprint studying injectivity
							questions arising from symmetric-polynomial
							maps on integer partitions.
						</p>

					</div>


					<div class="hero-actions">

						<a
							class="primary-action"
							href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.00420"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							View on arXiv ↗
						</a>

						<a
							href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.00420"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							Read PDF ↗
						</a>

					</div>

				</div>

			</section>


			<!-- ==================================================
			     01 — RESEARCH QUESTION
			================================================== -->

			<section class="paper-section">

				<div class="section-label">
					01 — The Question
				</div>


				<div class="section-content">

					<p class="eyebrow">
						Research question
					</p>


					<h2>
						When do symmetric-polynomial constructions uniquely determine a partition?
					</h2>


					<div class="text-columns">

						<p>
							Ballantine, Beck and Merca introduced an
							elementary symmetric partition map that sends
							a partition to another partition constructed
							from the summands appearing in an elementary
							symmetric polynomial.
						</p>


						<p>
							A natural question is whether this transformation
							preserves enough information to uniquely recover
							the original partition—in other words, whether
							the map is injective.
						</p>

					</div>

				</div>

			</section>


			<!-- ==================================================
			     02 — CONTEXT
			================================================== -->

			<section class="paper-section">

				<div class="section-label">
					02 — Context
				</div>


				<div class="section-content">

					<p class="eyebrow">
						From conjecture to counterexample
					</p>


					<h2>
						A refined conjecture remained open.
					</h2>


					<div class="context-flow">

						<div class="context-item">

							<span>
								01
							</span>

							<div>

								<h3>
									Original conjecture
								</h3>

								<p>
									The elementary symmetric partition map
									was conjectured to be injective for
									partitions whose length is at least
									the symmetric-polynomial degree.
								</p>

							</div>

						</div>


						<div class="context-item">

							<span>
								02
							</span>

							<div>

								<h3>
									First obstruction
								</h3>

								<p>
									The boundary case where the partition
									length equals the degree was previously
									shown to fail.
								</p>

							</div>

						</div>


						<div class="context-item">

							<span>
								03
							</span>

							<div>

								<h3>
									Refined conjecture
								</h3>

								<p>
									This led to a weaker conjecture:
									perhaps injectivity still holds once
									the partition length is strictly larger.
								</p>

							</div>

						</div>


						<div class="context-item">

							<span>
								04
							</span>

							<div>

								<h3>
									Our question
								</h3>

								<p>
									Does this refined injectivity statement
									actually survive?
								</p>

							</div>

						</div>

					</div>

				</div>

			</section>


			<!-- ==================================================
			     03 — MAIN RESULTS
			================================================== -->

			<section class="paper-section">

				<div class="section-label">
					03 — Main Results
				</div>


				<div class="section-content">

					<p class="eyebrow">
						Two complementary results
					</p>


					<h2>
						One map fails.<br />
						Its analogue succeeds.
					</h2>


					<div class="result-grid">

						<!-- RESULT 01 -->

						<div class="result-card">

							<div class="result-top">

								<span>
									Result 01
								</span>

								<span>
									Counterexample
								</span>

							</div>


							<h3>
								The refined injectivity conjecture is false.
							</h3>


							<p class="result-description">
								We prove that the elementary symmetric partition map
								is not injective on partitions of length&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-inline">2j</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;for every&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-inline">j ≥ 3</span>.
							</p>


							<div class="math-line">

								<span class="math-symbol">
									pre<sub>j</sub>
								</span>

								<span class="math-statement">
									is not injective when
								</span>

								<span class="math-symbol">
									ℓ = 2j, &nbsp; j ≥ 3
								</span>

							</div>

						</div>


						<!-- RESULT 02 -->

						<div class="result-card">

							<div class="result-top">

								<span>
									Result 02
								</span>

								<span>
									Positive theorem
								</span>

							</div>


							<h3>
								The complete homogeneous analogue is injective.
							</h3>


							<p class="result-description">
								For the analogous map defined using the complete
								homogeneous symmetric polynomial, we prove
								injectivity on the set of
								<span class="emphasis-inline">all partitions</span>.
							</p>


							<div class="math-line">

								<span class="math-symbol">
									prh<sub>j</sub>
								</span>

								<span class="math-statement">
									is injective on all partitions
								</span>

							</div>

						</div>

					</div>

				</div>

			</section>


			<!-- ==================================================
			     04 — SUMMARY
			================================================== -->

			<section class="paper-section">

				<div class="section-label">
					04 — Summary
				</div>


				<div class="section-content">

					<p class="eyebrow">
						In brief
					</p>


					<h2>
						Counterexamples reveal a sharp contrast between two natural partition maps.
					</h2>


					<div class="summary-layout">

						<div class="summary-lead">

							<p>
								The paper shows that increasing the
								partition length beyond the previously
								known failure case does not restore
								injectivity for the elementary symmetric
								construction.
							</p>

						</div>


						<div class="summary-detail">

							<p>
								At the same time, replacing the elementary
								symmetric polynomial with the complete
								homogeneous symmetric polynomial produces
								a fundamentally different outcome:
								the corresponding map is injective
								without a restriction on partition length.
							</p>

							<p>
								Together, the results illustrate how
								closely related symmetric-polynomial
								constructions can encode partition
								information in very different ways.
							</p>

						</div>

					</div>

				</div>

			</section>


